                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "c12670dc-3e0f-4173-ad78-d421ba3b9493",
                        "source_entry_id": "2a59798b-f1ff-373d-92f6-99c45400aaa9",
                        "source_sense_id": "24ccdce0-40e7-36bf-8962-8a891ebbd006",
                        "target_lang_word": "eutrophication",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "eutrophication",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "27d69f59-58b0-3584-b359-b959b981357b",
                                        "entry_id": "480809de-eed3-3df0-8f6e-709e45293fd9",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The process of becoming eutrophic; the ecosystem's response to the addition of artificial or natural nutrients, mainly phosphates, through detergents, fertilizers, or sewage, to an aquatic system."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "62dd174e-820c-4c3f-b63e-fe8dee4c3ce6",
                                                "sense_id": "27d69f59-58b0-3584-b359-b959b981357b",
                                                "text": "I already hinted at the problem of sea eutrophication (where excess nutrients cause algae to flourish) due to the phosphates that used to be added to washing powder, and, surely, it is not pleasant for plants to find underfoot (or, better, \"underroot\") those surfactants we use daily at home (please, never pour your dishwater on the roots of a magnificent camellia, even if it is blooming in the garden of a next-door neighbor you cannot stand)."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "26b41999-d191-4af6-b141-54eed145bf68",
                                                "sense_id": "27d69f59-58b0-3584-b359-b959b981357b",
                                                "text": "Amphisteginids and peneroplids were among the few taxa found in the bay environments, probably due to their preferences for phytal substrates and tolerance to moderate levels of eutrophication."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "30bd3ceb-5652-40f9-b589-24899e98c40a",
                        "source_entry_id": "2a59798b-f1ff-373d-92f6-99c45400aaa9",
                        "source_sense_id": "24ccdce0-40e7-36bf-8962-8a891ebbd006",
                        "target_lang_word": "eutrophisation",
                        "target_lang_code": "fr",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "eutrophisation",
                                "target_lang_code": "fr",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "39b76aca-8a1e-33aa-ba04-6b7d4d440e0f",
                                        "entry_id": "3e5a4d4e-cedb-3b57-9f57-4efabd1f2ef9",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Processus par lequel des nutriments s’accumulent dans un milieu ou un habitat (terrestre ou aquatique)."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "Écologie aquatique",
                                            "Hydrobiologie",
                                            "Sciences de l’environnement"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "21db6c8c-22a2-41d0-b1d7-4c2bdeeaacb9",
                                                "sense_id": "39b76aca-8a1e-33aa-ba04-6b7d4d440e0f",
                                                "text": "Il existe plusieurs méthodes pour évaluer la contribution d’un système à l’eutrophisation."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3ff19f23-9ac8-4f91-8edf-c7d0bcd19148",
                                                "sense_id": "39b76aca-8a1e-33aa-ba04-6b7d4d440e0f",
                                                "text": "Par exemple la mer peut être victime d’eutrophisation (marées vertes) au-delà de la dizaine de milligrammes [de nitrates]."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "180cde19-3f2b-4f8d-b59e-c035512e90fd",
                                                "sense_id": "39b76aca-8a1e-33aa-ba04-6b7d4d440e0f",
                                                "text": "On considère que l’eutrophisation survient lorsque la production primaire dépasse 300 g C/m2/an."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "02fda96b-4e0e-41d1-8cbb-34e528c71051",
                                                "sense_id": "39b76aca-8a1e-33aa-ba04-6b7d4d440e0f",
                                                "text": "Techniquement, les scientifiques appellent le phénomène l’eutrophisation, et sont tous d’accord pour dire que ce développement invasif d’algues en plans d’eau près des côtes est essentiellement dû aux nitrates et aux phosphates."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
